1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:03,000
Every cloud application moves through the same four phases.

2
00:00:03,000 --> 00:00:07,000
Development, implementation, operation, and maintenance.

3
00:00:07,000 --> 00:00:09,000
This lesson frames all four,

4
00:00:09,000 --> 00:00:11,000
then zooms in on the first two,

5
00:00:11,000 --> 00:00:14,000
where an idea becomes shipped software.

6
00:00:14,000 --> 00:00:16,000
We'll prototype against the messages API,

7
00:00:16,000 --> 00:00:18,000
then wire up off,

8
00:00:18,000 --> 00:00:21,000
error handling, and deployment. Let's dive in.

9
00:00:21,000 --> 00:00:24,000
Let's get the whole map in view before we go deep.

10
00:00:24,000 --> 00:00:27,000
A cloud application lives through four phases.

11
00:00:27,000 --> 00:00:31,000
In develop, you prototype your prompts and integrations

12
00:00:31,000 --> 00:00:32,000
against the messages API,

13
00:00:32,000 --> 00:00:35,000
just proving the idea can work.

14
00:00:35,000 --> 00:00:37,000
In implement, you wire in auth, secrets,

15
00:00:37,000 --> 00:00:39,000
error handling, and deployment,

16
00:00:39,000 --> 00:00:43,000
so that prototype runs in its real target environment.

17
00:00:43,000 --> 00:00:45,000
In operate, the app is live,

18
00:00:45,000 --> 00:00:47,000
and you're watching latency, token usage,

19
00:00:47,000 --> 00:00:51,000
error rates, and any refusal or failure signatures.

20
00:00:51,000 --> 00:00:54,000
And in maintain, you migrate models,

21
00:00:54,000 --> 00:00:56,000
version your prompts and schemas,

22
00:00:56,000 --> 00:00:58,000
and regression test every upgrade.

23
00:00:58,000 --> 00:01:03,000
Four phases, and each one asks something different of you as the builder.

24
00:01:03,000 --> 00:01:05,000
Today, we're focused on the first two.

25
00:01:06,000 --> 00:01:08,000
So let's start in the develop phase,

26
00:01:08,000 --> 00:01:12,000
where the whole point is learning, not polishing.

27
00:01:12,000 --> 00:01:16,000
You prototype your prompts directly against the messages API

28
00:01:16,000 --> 00:01:18,000
and see what cloud actually returns.

29
00:01:18,000 --> 00:01:20,000
You wire up a rough integration,

30
00:01:20,000 --> 00:01:22,000
just enough to confirm the shape of the response

31
00:01:22,000 --> 00:01:24,000
is what your app needs.

32
00:01:24,000 --> 00:01:25,000
And you iterate on the prompt,

33
00:01:25,000 --> 00:01:27,000
testing it against real inputs

34
00:01:27,000 --> 00:01:30,000
and the messy adversarial ones too,

35
00:01:30,000 --> 00:01:33,000
because one lucky output isn't proof it works.

36
00:01:33,000 --> 00:01:36,000
What you're deliberately not doing yet is hardening.

37
00:01:36,000 --> 00:01:40,000
No elaborate error handling, no production configuration.

38
00:01:40,000 --> 00:01:45,000
Speed and learning matter more than polish right now.

39
00:01:45,000 --> 00:01:46,000
Get the idea proven first,

40
00:01:46,000 --> 00:01:50,000
and save the hardening for the implement phase that follows.

41
00:01:51,000 --> 00:01:53,000
Let's look at what actually goes into a request,

42
00:01:54,000 --> 00:01:55,000
because there are three pieces

43
00:01:55,000 --> 00:01:57,000
you'll set on every single call.

44
00:01:57,000 --> 00:01:59,000
First, the endpoint.

45
00:01:59,000 --> 00:02:03,000
Every call is an HTTP post to the V1 messages endpoint,

46
00:02:03,000 --> 00:02:06,000
and you send JSON and get JSON back.

47
00:02:06,000 --> 00:02:09,000
So this is a plain REST API underneath.

48
00:02:09,000 --> 00:02:12,000
Second, the XAPI key header.

49
00:02:12,000 --> 00:02:13,000
That's what authenticates you,

50
00:02:13,000 --> 00:02:16,000
and you keep that key in a secret manager.

51
00:02:16,000 --> 00:02:18,000
Never paste it into your code.

52
00:02:18,000 --> 00:02:21,000
Third, the anthropic version header.

53
00:02:21,000 --> 00:02:23,000
It's required, and it pins the request

54
00:02:24,000 --> 00:02:27,000
and response behavior to a dated API version,

55
00:02:27,000 --> 00:02:29,000
so your integration doesn't shift under you

56
00:02:29,000 --> 00:02:31,000
when the API evolves.

57
00:02:31,000 --> 00:02:32,000
Endpoint key version.

58
00:02:32,000 --> 00:02:35,000
Get those three right, and you're talking to Claude.

59
00:02:35,000 --> 00:02:37,000
Here's a distinction worth holding onto,

60
00:02:37,000 --> 00:02:41,000
because develop and implement pull in opposite directions.

61
00:02:41,000 --> 00:02:45,000
When you prototype, you move fast and accept rough edges.

62
00:02:45,000 --> 00:02:48,000
You try different prompts and tweak the wording,

63
00:02:48,000 --> 00:02:50,000
all because the only goal is proving

64
00:02:50,000 --> 00:02:52,000
the idea can work at all.

65
00:02:52,000 --> 00:02:56,000
Then, as you move toward implement, you harden.

66
00:02:56,000 --> 00:02:57,000
You lock the pieces down.

67
00:02:57,000 --> 00:03:01,000
You add input validation, real error handling,

68
00:03:01,000 --> 00:03:04,000
retries for flaky calls, and stable configuration,

69
00:03:04,000 --> 00:03:07,000
so the same request behaves predictably

70
00:03:07,000 --> 00:03:08,000
every time you run it.

71
00:03:08,000 --> 00:03:11,000
Same code, two mindsets.

72
00:03:11,000 --> 00:03:14,000
Prototype asks, can this work?

73
00:03:14,000 --> 00:03:16,000
Harden asks, will this keep working under load

74
00:03:16,000 --> 00:03:19,000
on a bad day in production?

75
00:03:19,000 --> 00:03:21,000
You need both, just not at the same moment.

76
00:03:22,000 --> 00:03:26,000
Now, the implement phase, where that proven prototype

77
00:03:26,000 --> 00:03:29,000
becomes something that runs in the real world.

78
00:03:29,000 --> 00:03:31,000
The theme of this phase is durability,

79
00:03:31,000 --> 00:03:34,000
taking something that worked once on your machine

80
00:03:34,000 --> 00:03:37,000
and making it run reliably, unattended,

81
00:03:37,000 --> 00:03:38,000
everywhere it needs to.

82
00:03:38,000 --> 00:03:40,000
Think about what actually changes

83
00:03:40,000 --> 00:03:42,000
when the code leaves your laptop.

84
00:03:42,000 --> 00:03:46,000
On your machine, the API key sat in an environment variable.

85
00:03:46,000 --> 00:03:49,000
You exported by hand, and when a call failed,

86
00:03:49,000 --> 00:03:51,000
you just ran it again.

87
00:03:51,000 --> 00:03:54,000
In production, nobody is there to rerun anything.

88
00:03:54,000 --> 00:03:57,000
The app has to load its own credentials,

89
00:03:57,000 --> 00:03:59,000
survive a rate limit at two in the morning,

90
00:03:59,000 --> 00:04:03,000
and behave the same in staging as it does in production.

91
00:04:03,000 --> 00:04:06,000
Every manual step you performed while prototyping

92
00:04:06,000 --> 00:04:09,000
has to become something the system does for itself.

93
00:04:09,000 --> 00:04:11,000
That's the checklist we'll wire up next.

94
00:04:12,000 --> 00:04:15,000
Let's make the implement phase concrete

95
00:04:15,000 --> 00:04:18,000
with the three things you'll wire up almost every time.

96
00:04:18,000 --> 00:04:20,000
One, auth and secrets.

97
00:04:20,000 --> 00:04:23,000
Store your API key in a secret manager

98
00:04:23,000 --> 00:04:27,000
and load it at runtime and never commit it to the repo

99
00:04:27,000 --> 00:04:30,000
because a leaked key is a real incident.

100
00:04:30,000 --> 00:04:32,000
Two, error handling.

101
00:04:32,000 --> 00:04:34,000
Catch rate limit and server errors.

102
00:04:34,000 --> 00:04:37,000
Back off and retry so a single hiccup

103
00:04:37,000 --> 00:04:40,000
doesn't crash the whole request.

104
00:04:40,000 --> 00:04:41,000
Three, deployment.

105
00:04:41,000 --> 00:04:45,000
Package the app, set its configuration for each environment,

106
00:04:45,000 --> 00:04:48,000
and deploy it to your target platform.

107
00:04:48,000 --> 00:04:50,000
Auth errors deployment.

108
00:04:50,000 --> 00:04:52,000
None of it is glamorous, but this is the plumbing

109
00:04:52,000 --> 00:04:54,000
that turns a working prototype

110
00:04:54,000 --> 00:04:57,000
into something you can actually put in front of users.

111
00:04:58,000 --> 00:05:02,000
Let's zoom in on errors because at the integration edge,

112
00:05:02,000 --> 00:05:04,000
every network call can fail.

113
00:05:04,000 --> 00:05:07,000
The 429 and the 500 series errors,

114
00:05:07,000 --> 00:05:10,000
you can already triage from the architecture lessons.

115
00:05:10,000 --> 00:05:14,000
Both sit in the retry bucket, so back off and resend.

116
00:05:14,000 --> 00:05:17,000
The new case in the implement phase is the 401.

117
00:05:17,000 --> 00:05:20,000
That means your authentication is bad,

118
00:05:20,000 --> 00:05:22,000
a missing or wrong key,

119
00:05:22,000 --> 00:05:24,000
and no amount of retrying will fix it.

120
00:05:24,000 --> 00:05:26,000
You have to correct the credentials,

121
00:05:26,000 --> 00:05:30,000
and until you do, every call fails the same way.

122
00:05:30,000 --> 00:05:31,000
That's why a well-built edge

123
00:05:31,000 --> 00:05:34,000
surfaces an auth failure immediately

124
00:05:34,000 --> 00:05:37,000
instead of letting it hide inside a retry loop.

125
00:05:37,000 --> 00:05:39,000
Triage stays the skill.

126
00:05:39,000 --> 00:05:42,000
Retry what clears, surface, and fix what doesn't.

127
00:05:43,000 --> 00:05:45,000
One more implement phase habit,

128
00:05:45,000 --> 00:05:47,000
and it's about version control.

129
00:05:48,000 --> 00:05:49,000
Some of your configuration belongs in Git,

130
00:05:49,000 --> 00:05:51,000
and some deliberately doesn't.

131
00:05:51,000 --> 00:05:54,000
Your shared config, your Claw.md,

132
00:05:54,000 --> 00:05:58,000
and the project settings.json gets committed,

133
00:05:58,000 --> 00:06:00,000
so every teammate and every environment

134
00:06:00,000 --> 00:06:02,000
picks up the same behavior.

135
00:06:02,000 --> 00:06:06,000
Your personal config, the settings.local.json

136
00:06:06,000 --> 00:06:09,000
stays Git-ignored because it holds

137
00:06:09,000 --> 00:06:12,000
machine-specific overrides that are yours alone.

138
00:06:12,000 --> 00:06:14,000
And here's the one people forget,

139
00:06:14,000 --> 00:06:18,000
pin an exact model ID and commit that too.

140
00:06:18,000 --> 00:06:22,000
On pre-4.6 models, that means the dated snapshot

141
00:06:22,000 --> 00:06:24,000
rather than the shorter floating alias.

142
00:06:24,000 --> 00:06:29,000
From 4.6 on, the dateless ID like Clawed Opus 4.8

143
00:06:29,000 --> 00:06:31,000
already is the pinned snapshot,

144
00:06:31,000 --> 00:06:33,000
so there is no alias to avoid.

145
00:06:33,000 --> 00:06:36,000
That way, a teammate's run and production's run

146
00:06:36,000 --> 00:06:39,000
behave like yours instead of silently drifting

147
00:06:39,000 --> 00:06:41,000
when a new model ships.

148
00:06:41,000 --> 00:06:43,000
Reproducible behavior starts with putting

149
00:06:43,000 --> 00:06:49,000
the right configuration under source control. Let's recap.

150
00:06:49,000 --> 00:06:51,000
Develop and implement carry an idea

151
00:06:51,000 --> 00:06:54,000
from prototype all the way to shipped software.

152
00:06:54,000 --> 00:06:58,000
Develop proves it against the messages API.

153
00:06:58,000 --> 00:07:00,000
Implement wires in auth, secrets,

154
00:07:00,000 --> 00:07:02,000
error handling, and deployment,

155
00:07:02,000 --> 00:07:04,000
and handles failures at the edge.

156
00:07:04,000 --> 00:07:07,000
Just remember, commit your shared config

157
00:07:07,000 --> 00:07:12,000
and pin an exact model ID so behavior stays reproducible.

158
00:07:12,000 --> 00:07:14,000
That covers the develop and implement phases.

159
00:07:14,000 --> 00:07:17,000
Take a breath, and I'll see you in the next one.

